  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: 180d0828f9cf423459360235cd6922f2, type: 3}
  m_Name: Lungs
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: Assembly-CSharp::DeepVisionTutorialSO
  steps:
  - The lungs are paired respiratory organs occupying most of the thoracic cavity.
    Their primary function is gas exchange, supplying oxygen to the bloodstream while
    removing carbon dioxide generated by cellular metabolism.
  - The right lung contains four lobes while the left lung contains three, allowing
    efficient expansion within the thoracic cavity. Elastic lung tissue expands during
    inhalation and recoils during exhalation.
  - Millions of microscopic alveoli line the terminal respiratory passages. These
    thin-walled air sacs provide a massive surface area where oxygen diffuses into
    pulmonary capillaries and carbon dioxide diffuses outward for expiration.
  - The diaphragm is the primary respiratory muscle separating the thoracic and abdominal
    cavities. During contraction it enlarges thoracic volume, creating negative pressure
    that draws air into the lungs.
  - Pulmonary circulation carries deoxygenated blood from the right side of the heart
    into the lungs through pulmonary arteries. Oxygenated blood then returns to the
    left atrium through pulmonary veins for systemic distribution.
